Title:
Full writeup of the Windows GDID (Global Device Identifier) — reverse engineering

Type:
GitHub Tool (research write-up / documentation)

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This repo is a reverse-engineering write-up explaining what Microsoft’s “Global Device Identifier (GDID)” is and how it’s generated, stored, and propagated in Windows 11.  
- It debunks viral claims that GDID is a 128-bit hardware-serial-derived fingerprint, showing instead that it is a 64-bit Microsoft Account device identifier (Device PUID) that changes on Windows reinstall.  
- The analysis traces the end-to-end chain: `wlidsvc.dll` provisions the device with `login.live.com` and receives a server-assigned Device PUID, persists it in registry, and the Connected Devices Platform (`cdp.dll` / `CDPSvc`) consumes (does not compute) that ID to register the device into Microsoft’s Device Directory Service (DDS) graph.  
- It shows where GDID surfaces publicly (Delivery Optimization / Update Compliance `UCDOStatus.GlobalDeviceId`) and provides a simple registry query to retrieve your own value.  
- Methodology combines static RE with public PDB symbols and live ETW TraceLogging captures to reproduce the registration flow and endpoints.  
- Useful for red teamers, malware analysts, and defenders who need to understand Windows device identity/telemetry linkability and attribution implications.

Technical Focus:
- Windows identity stack internals (`wlidsvc.dll`, MSA/Passport provisioning)
- Connected Devices Platform (CDP) and DDS device graph registration (`cdp.dll`, `CDPSvc`)
- ETW/TraceLogging capture and analysis of CDP providers
- Registry persistence of device identifiers (`HKCU\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\IdentityCRL\...`)
- Delivery Optimization / Update Compliance telemetry (`UCDOStatus.GlobalDeviceId`)
- Server-assigned Device PUID format and `g:<decimal>` representation

Use Cases:
- Map Windows device identity artifacts for attribution/OPSEC risk assessments
- Hunt for or validate presence of GDID/Device PUID in endpoint telemetry pipelines
- Reproduce and monitor CDP/DDS registration behavior via ETW for detection engineering
- Identify registry locations containing stable device identifiers for forensics/IR
- Evaluate mitigation options (disabling CDP/Activity History) to reduce cross-device linkage

Keywords:
Windows 11, GDID, GlobalDeviceId, Device PUID, Passport Unique ID, Microsoft Account, wlidsvc.dll, cdp.dll, CDPSvc, CDPUserSvc, Device Directory Service, DDS, Delivery Optimization, UCDOStatus, Update Compliance, ETW, TraceLogging, public PDB symbols, IdentityCRL registry, login.live.com, activity.windows.com, dds.microsoft.com